WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.106 --> 00:00:06.883
High above the city, on a tall column, stood the statue of the Happy Prince.

2
00:00:06.883 --> 00:00:18.219
He was gilded all over with thin leaves of fine gold, for eyes he had two bright sapphires, and a large red ruby glowed on his sword hilt.

3
00:00:18.219 --> 00:00:21.654
He was very much admired indeed.

4
00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:31.305
"He is as beautiful as a weathercock," remarked one of the Town Councilors, who wished to gain a reputation for having artistic tastes.

5
00:00:31.305 --> 00:00:39.478
"Only not quite so useful," he added, fearing lest people should think him unpractical, which he really was not.

6
00:00:39.479 --> 00:00:47.151
"Why can't you be like the Happy Prince?" asked a sensible mother of her little boy, who was crying for the moon.

7
00:00:47.152 --> 00:00:50.858
"The Happy Prince never dreams of crying for anything."

8
00:00:50.858 --> 00:01:00.030
"I am glad there is someone in the world who is quite happy," muttered a disappointed man as he gazed at the wonderful statue.

9
00:01:00.031 --> 00:01:10.451
"He looks just like an angel," said the Charity Children as they came out of the cathedral in their bright, scarlet cloaks and their clean, white pinafores.

10
00:01:10.452 --> 00:01:15.609
"How do you know?" said the Master. "You have never seen one."

11
00:01:15.609 --> 00:01:20.414
"Ah, but we have, in our dreams," answered the children.

12
00:01:20.414 --> 00:01:27.216
And the Master frowned and looked very severe, for he did not approve of children dreaming.

13
00:01:27.217 --> 00:01:32.555
One night, there flew over the city a little Swallow.

14
00:01:32.555 --> 00:01:41.862
His friends had gone away to Egypt six weeks before, but he had stayed behind, for he was in love with the most beautiful Reed.

15
00:01:41.862 --> 00:01:54.322
He had met her early in the spring as he was flying down the river after a big yellow moth, and had been so attracted by her slender waist that he had stopped to talk to her.

16
00:01:54.322 --> 00:02:02.919
"Shall I love you?" said the Swallow, who liked to come to the point at once, and the Reed made him a low bow.

17
00:02:02.919 --> 00:02:11.228
So he flew round and round her, touching the water with his wings and making silver ripples.

18
00:02:11.228 --> 00:02:15.812
This was his courtship, and it lasted all through the summer.

19
00:02:15.812 --> 00:02:20.501
"It is a ridiculous attachment," twittered the other Swallows.

20
00:02:20.501 --> 00:02:28.930
"She has no money and far too many relations"; and indeed the river was quite full of Reeds.

21
00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:33.642
Then, when the autumn came, they all flew away.

22
00:02:33.642 --> 00:02:39.769
After they had gone, he felt lonely and began to tire of his ladylove.

23
00:02:39.769 --> 00:02:47.697
"She has no conversation," he said, "and I am afraid that she is always flirting with the wind."

24
00:02:47.697 --> 00:02:54.331
And certainly, whenever the wind blew, the Reed made the most graceful curtsies.

25
00:02:54.331 --> 00:03:05.606
"I admit that she is domestic," he continued, "but I love traveling, and my wife, consequently, should love traveling also."

26
00:03:05.606 --> 00:03:14.653
"Will you come away with me?" he said finally to her; but the Reed shook her head. She was so attached to her home.

27
00:03:14.653 --> 00:03:24.137
"You have been trifling with me," he cried. "I am off to the Pyramids. Good-bye!" and he flew away.

28
00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:29.650
All day long, he flew, and at nighttime, he arrived at the city.

29
00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:35.963
"Where shall I put up?" he said. "I hope the town has made preparations."

30
00:03:35.963 --> 00:03:40.176
Then he saw the statue on the tall column.

31
00:03:40.179 --> 00:03:48.741
"I will put up there," he cried. "It is a fine position, with plenty of fresh air."

32
00:03:48.741 --> 00:03:53.669
So he alighted just between the feet of the Happy Prince.

33
00:03:53.669 --> 00:04:10.330
"I have a golden bedroom," he said softly to himself as he looked round, and he prepared to go to sleep; but just as he was putting his head under his wing, a large drop of water fell on him.

34
00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:13.889
"What a curious thing!" he cried.

35
00:04:13.889 --> 00:04:21.795
"There is not a single cloud in the sky, the stars are quite clear and bright, and yet it is raining.

36
00:04:21.795 --> 00:04:25.531
The climate in the north of Europe is really dreadful.

37
00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:30.694
The Reed used to like the rain, but that was merely her selfishness."

38
00:04:30.695 --> 00:04:33.574
Then, another drop fell.

39
00:04:33.574 --> 00:04:38.392
"What is the use of a statue if it cannot keep the rain off?" he said.

40
00:04:38.392 --> 00:04:43.584
"I must look for a good chimney-pot," and he determined to fly away.

41
00:04:43.584 --> 00:04:52.973
But before he had opened his wings, a third drop fell, and he looked up and saw—Ah! What did he see?

42
00:04:52.973 --> 00:05:00.692
The eyes of the Happy Prince were filled with tears, and tears were running down his golden cheeks.

43
00:05:00.692 --> 00:05:07.483
His face was so beautiful in the moonlight that the little Swallow was filled with pity.

44
00:05:07.483 --> 00:05:09.839
"Who are you?" he said.

45
00:05:09.839 --> 00:05:12.564
"I am the Happy Prince."

46
00:05:12.564 --> 00:05:18.636
"Why are you weeping then?" asked the Swallow. "You have quite drenched me."

47
00:05:18.636 --> 00:05:33.719
"When I was alive and had a human heart," answered the statue, "I did not know what tears were, for I lived in the Palace of Sans-Souci, where sorrow is not allowed to enter.

48
00:05:33.719 --> 00:05:43.171
In the daytime, I played with my companions in the garden, and in the evening, I led the dance in the Great Hall.

49
00:05:43.171 --> 00:05:54.136
Round the garden ran a very lofty wall, but I never cared to ask what lay beyond it; everything about me was so beautiful.

50
00:05:54.136 --> 00:06:03.613
My courtiers called me the Happy Prince, and happy indeed I was, if pleasure be happiness.

51
00:06:03.613 --> 00:06:07.404
So I lived, and so I died.

52
00:06:07.404 --> 00:06:25.317
And now that I am dead, they have set me up here so high that I can see all the ugliness and all the misery of my city, and though my heart is made of lead, yet I cannot choose but weep."

53
00:06:25.317 --> 00:06:30.699
"What! Is he not solid gold?" said the Swallow to himself.

54
00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:34.977
He was too polite to make any personal remarks out loud.

55
00:06:34.977 --> 00:06:47.590
"Far away," continued the statue in a low musical voice, "far away in a little street, there is a poor house.

56
00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:54.804
One of the windows is open, and through it, I can see a woman seated at a table.

57
00:06:54.804 --> 00:07:05.558
Her face is thin and worn, and she has coarse, red hands, all pricked by the needle, for she is a seamstress.

58
00:07:05.558 --> 00:07:16.349
She is embroidering passionflowers on a satin gown for the loveliest of the Queen's maids-of-honor to wear at the next Court Ball.

59
00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:22.198
In a bed in the corner of the room, her little boy is lying ill.

60
00:07:22.198 --> 00:07:26.174
He has a fever and is asking for oranges.

61
00:07:26.174 --> 00:07:32.230
His mother has nothing to give him but river water, so he is crying.

62
00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:41.245
Swallow, Swallow, little Swallow, will you not bring her the ruby out of my sword hilt?

63
00:07:41.246 --> 00:07:46.070
My feet are fastened to this pedestal, and I cannot move."

64
00:07:46.071 --> 00:07:49.868
"I am waited for in Egypt," said the Swallow.

65
00:07:49.868 --> 00:07:56.033
"My friends are flying up and down the Nile and talking to the large lotus flowers.

66
00:07:56.043 --> 00:08:04.305
Soon, they will go to sleep in the tomb of the great King. The King is there himself in his painted coffin.

67
00:08:04.306 --> 00:08:08.686
He is wrapped in yellow linen and embalmed with spices.

68
00:08:08.687 --> 00:08:16.436
Round his neck is a chain of pale green jade, and his hands are like withered leaves."

